mode: "100644"
text: "# The reality of quote cards & text graphics in 2026 (verify-quarterly)\n\nVolatile figures — attribute, mark **verify-quarterly**. The craft principles (quotability first; one idea,\noutsized; mobile legibility; honest attribution) are stable typography + integrity fundamentals.\n\n## Why the format works (and its ceiling)\n- A quote card packages **one idea** into a self-contained, save-worthy mini-billboard — the format over-indexes\n  on **saves and re-shares** because people repost a thought they want to be seen holding.\n- Marketing sources report quote/visual posts lift engagement meaningfully (e.g. \"~150% vs plain text\", \"visuals\n  ~94% more views\") — treat these as **directional and unverified**; attribute, verify-quarterly. The reliable\n  point: **the design amplifies a strong line; it can't create one.** A mundane sentence in a beautiful font is\n  still mundane.\n\n## Quotability (the whole game)\n- **Not every sentence earns a card.** The test: does the line **stand alone** and make someone stop/save/share?\n  Favor a bold claim, a reframe, a sticky truth — and **shorter is better** (more room to breathe; it shouldn't\n  read like a paragraph).\n\n## Typography & legibility (well-established craft)\n- **Mobile-first:** ~80%+ of social consumption is mobile, so design for thumb-size — **min ~24pt** main quote\n  text, **high contrast**, safe margins, and **not text over a busy image** (add an overlay/solid panel).\n  **Test on an actual phone** before publishing.\n- **Two fonts max** (one is often enough) — one for the quote, one for attribution/accent; more dilutes impact.\n- **Hierarchy:** one line/key phrase leads (size/weight/contrast); **break lines where the meaning stays strong**,\n  never mid-word to fit; whitespace makes it feel premium.\n- **Consistency across a set** = brand recognition (route the system to `design-and-templates`).\n\n## The honesty spine (stable — sharp for this format)\nA quote card is an **attribution machine**, and quote cards *recontextualize* a line by design — which makes\nintegrity central:\n- **Never fabricate a quote** or attribute an invented/fictional line to a real person (the rule against\n  fictional quotes from real public figures). A misattributed quote card is **misinformation with nice\n  typography.**\n- **Never strip context** to change a quote's meaning; **verify the exact wording + source.**\n- **Consent** for a person's words/face (real or AI lookalike); customer quotes follow the social-proof/FTC rules;\n  a stat on a card must be **real + sourced**; don't reproduce **copyrighted** lyrics/poems.\n\n## House metrics\nSaves/shares (the format's superpower) + reach + profile visits — native only; never fabricate a quote, source,\nor stat.\n"
